# University Physics Volume 1, chapters 10-13

§ 1 10.0
N S rigid | Rigid body on a fixed axis | θ(t) |
d A body whose particles keep their mutual distances, turning about a fixed axis: every particle moves on a circle and one angle describes them all.
m one angle θ(t) ∈ S¹ (one degree of freedom); each particle on a circle of fixed radius r_j
g rigid body
R[def] rigid <- sys, circ : |r⃗_i − r⃗_j| fixed;  each particle on a circle about the axis | Constrained system of particles | Rigidity reduces N trajectories to one rotation angle.

§ 1 10.1
N O theta | Angular position | θ = s/r |
d Angle of a reference line of the body from a fixed direction, in radians: arc length over radius.
g angular position
R[read] theta <- rigid : θ = s/r | Angle of the body | The single coordinate of the rotation.
N O angvel | Angular velocity | ω⃗ = dθ/dt |
d Rate of change of angular position; as a vector it points along the axis by the right-hand rule. Tangential speed v_t = rω.
g angular velocity; instantaneous angular velocity
R[def] angvel <- theta, ddt : ω = dθ/dt | Rate of rotation | Same for every point of the rigid body.
R[gen] angvel <- omega, cross : ω⃗ along the axis, v⃗ = ω⃗ × r⃗ | Angular frequency as a vector | The angular frequency of circular motion, given a direction.
R[thm] speed <- angvel : v_t = r ω | Tangential speed | Points farther from the axis move faster.
N O alpha | Angular acceleration | α = dω/dt |
d Rate of change of angular velocity; tangential acceleration of a point is a_t = rα.
g angular acceleration; instantaneous angular acceleration
R[def] alpha <- angvel, ddt : α = dω/dt = d²θ/dt² | Rate of change of ω | The rotational analogue of acceleration.
R[thm] at <- alpha : a_t = r α | Tangential acceleration | A point at distance r speeds up along its circle at rα.

§ 1 10.2
N M rotkin | Rotational kinematics | θ = θ₀ + ω₀t + ½αt² |
d Equations for rotation at constant angular acceleration, the same form as the linear ones with θ, ω, α in place of x, v, a.
p valid for constant α; ω = ω₀ + αt; ω² = ω₀² + 2αΔθ; direct analogue of linear kinematics
g kinematics of rotational motion
R[analog] rotkin <- kin, alpha : x → θ, v → ω, a → α | Same mathematics | The derivation of the linear case applies line by line.

§ 1 10.3
X total linear acceleration :: = avec
R[thm] ac <- angvel : a_c = r ω² | Centripetal part for a rotating body | Each point of a rigid body has a centripetal acceleration rω².

§ 1 10.4
N O Krot | Rotational kinetic energy | K = ½Iω² |
d Kinetic energy of a rotating rigid body, the sum of ½m_j v_j² with v_j = r_jω.
g rotational kinetic energy
R[thm] Krot <- K, angvel, rigid : K = Σ ½ m_j (r_j ω)² = ½(Σ m_j r_j²) ω² | Sum over particles | The mass distribution enters only through Σ m_j r_j².
N O I | Moment of inertia | I = Σ m_j r_j² |
d Rotational inertia: how mass is distributed about the axis. Depends on the axis chosen.
g moment of inertia
R[def] I <- rigid, m : I = Σ m_j r_j²;  I = ∫ r² dm | Mass-weighted squared distance | Read off the rotational kinetic energy.
R[def] Krot <- I, angvel : K = ½ I ω² | Compact form | Kinetic energy in terms of I.

§ 1 10.5
X linear mass density :: = lmd
R[thm] I <- lmd, integ : I = ∫ r² λ dr | Continuous rod | e.g. ML²/12 about the centre of a thin rod.
N M paxis | Parallel-axis theorem | I = I_CM + Md² |
d The moment of inertia about any axis equals that about the parallel axis through the centre of mass plus Md².
p d = distance between the axes; I_CM is the minimum over parallel axes; moments of a compound body add
g parallel axis; parallel-axis theorem
R[thm] paxis <- I, rcm : Σ m_j |r⃗′_j + d⃗|² with Σ m_j r⃗′_j = 0 | Expand about the centre of mass | The cross term vanishes by the definition of the centre of mass.
N O smd | Surface mass density | σ = dm/dA |
d Mass per unit area of a thin sheet or disk.
g surface mass density
R[def] smd <- m, L : σ = dm/dA | Mass per area | Uniform disk: σ = M/(πR²).
R[thm] I <- smd, integ : I = ∫ r² σ dA | Continuous sheet | e.g. MR²/2 for a uniform disk.

§ 1 10.6
N O tau | Torque | τ⃗ = r⃗ × F⃗ |
d The turning effect of a force about a point or axis: force times lever arm, |τ| = r⊥F, signed by the right-hand rule.
g torque; lever arm
R[def] tau <- r, F, cross : τ⃗ = r⃗ × F⃗,  |τ| = r F sin θ = r⊥ F | Cross product | Only the force component perpendicular to r⃗ turns the body.

§ 1 10.7
N M n2rot | Second law for rotation | Στ = Iα |
d Net torque about a fixed axis equals moment of inertia times angular acceleration.
p analogue of F = ma; I depends on the axis; linear in the torques
g Newton’s second law for rotation; rotational dynamics
R[thm] n2rot <- n2, tau, I, rigid : Σ r_j F_j,⊥ = Σ m_j r_j² α | Second law for each particle | Multiply the tangential second law by r_j and sum.

§ 1 10.8
N O Wrot | Rotational work | W = ∫ τ dθ |
d Work done by a torque as the body turns.
g rotational work
R[thm] Wrot <- tau, theta, W, integ : dW = F⃗·dr⃗ = τ dθ | Work in angular variables | With dr = r dθ along the circle.
R[gen] wet <- Wrot, Krot : W_AB = K_B − K_A with W = ∫τ dθ, K = ½Iω² | Work–energy theorem for rotation | The same theorem in rotational variables.
R[thm] P <- tau, angvel : P = τ ω | Rotational power | dW/dt = τ dθ/dt.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Chapter 11
§ 1 11.1
N M roll | Rolling without slipping | v_CM = Rω |
d A wheel rolls without slipping when its contact point is instantaneously at rest; translation and rotation are then locked together.
p constraint v_CM = Rω, a_CM = Rα; static friction does no work; K = ½Mv_CM² + ½I_CMω²
g rolling motion
R[thm] roll <- rigid, rcm, angvel, fs : v_contact = v_CM − Rω = 0 | Contact point at rest | Static friction supplies the torque without sliding.

§ 1 11.2
N O Lang | Angular momentum | l⃗ = r⃗ × p⃗ |
d Moment of momentum about a point; for a rigid body rotating about a symmetry axis, L = Iω.
g angular momentum
R[def] Lang <- r, p, cross : l⃗ = r⃗ × p⃗;  L⃗ = Σ l⃗_j | Cross product | Depends on the chosen origin.
R[thm] Lang <- I, angvel : L = I ω | Rigid body | Summing r_j m_j v_j with v_j = r_jω.
N M dLdt | Torque law | dL⃗/dt = Στ⃗ |
d The rate of change of angular momentum equals the net external torque.
p rotational analogue of F = dp/dt; vector equation; internal torques cancel for central internal forces
R[thm] dLdt <- n2, Lang, tau : d(r⃗ × p⃗)/dt = v⃗ × p⃗ + r⃗ × F⃗ = τ⃗ | Differentiate r⃗ × p⃗ | v⃗ × m v⃗ = 0.

§ 1 11.3
N M Lcons | Conservation of angular momentum | L⃗ = const |
d With no net external torque, the total angular momentum is constant; a skater who pulls in her arms (smaller I) spins faster.
p vector law; requires zero net external torque; I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂ for a body changing shape
g law of conservation of angular momentum
R[thm] Lcons <- dLdt, n3 : Σ τ⃗_ext = 0 ⇒ dL⃗/dt = 0 | No external torque | Internal torques cancel in pairs.

§ 1 11.4
N O omegaP | Precession rate | ω_P = rMg/(Iω) |
d Slow turning of a spinning top's axis, because gravity's torque changes the direction of L⃗ rather than its size.
g precession
R[thm] omegaP <- dLdt, Lang, w : dL = τ dt with τ ⊥ L ⇒ ω_P = τ/L = rMg/(Iω) | Torque perpendicular to L | The angular momentum vector turns at rate τ/L.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Chapter 12
§ 1 12.1
N S staticeq | Static equilibrium | v⃗_CM = 0, ω⃗ = 0 |
d A rigid body at rest that stays at rest; it has no translational and no rotational acceleration.
m configuration of a rigid body with ΣF⃗ = 0 and Στ⃗ = 0 about every point
g equilibrium; static equilibrium
N M eqcond | Equilibrium conditions | ΣF⃗ = 0,  Στ⃗ = 0 |
d The first condition (zero net force) prevents translation; the second (zero net torque) prevents rotation.
p six scalar equations in 3D; if both hold, the torque condition holds about any point; necessary for static equilibrium
g first equilibrium condition; second equilibrium condition
R[thm] eqcond <- n2sys, dLdt : M a⃗_CM = ΣF⃗ = 0,  dL⃗/dt = Στ⃗ = 0 | Translational and rotational laws at rest | The two dynamical laws with all accelerations zero.
R[thm] staticeq <- eqcond, rigid : solve ΣF⃗ = 0, Στ⃗ = 0 for the unknown forces | Statics problem | The conditions determine the support forces.
N O rcg | Center of gravity | r⃗_CG |
d Point where the total weight can be taken to act; it coincides with the centre of mass in a uniform gravitational field.
g center of gravity; gravitational torque
R[thm] rcg <- rcm, w, tau : τ⃗_grav = r⃗_CM × M g⃗ | Gravitational torque | In uniform g⃗ the torques of the particle weights add up to the weight acting at the centre of mass.

§ 1 12.3
N O stress | Stress | F/A |
d Force per unit area that deforms a body: tensile or compressive (perpendicular), bulk (from all sides), shear (parallel to the surface).
g stress; tensile stress; compressive stress; bulk stress; shear stress
R[def] stress <- F, L : stress = F⊥/A (tensile, compressive),  F∥/A (shear) | Force per area | Area A = ℓ² of the surface the force acts on.
N O strain | Strain | ΔL/L₀ |
d Fractional deformation: change of length, volume or shear offset divided by the original size; dimensionless.
g strain; tensile strain; compressive strain; bulk strain; shear strain
R[def] strain <- L : ΔL/L₀ (tensile),  ΔV/V₀ (bulk),  Δx/L₀ (shear) | Fractional change | Ratios of lengths, so dimensionless.
N M elin | Linear elasticity | stress = modulus × strain |
d For small deformations stress is proportional to strain; the constant is the elastic modulus of the material for that kind of deformation.
p Hooke's law for materials; linear; reversible below the elastic limit; one modulus per deformation type
g elastic modulus
R[gen] elin <- hooke, stress, strain : F = −kx  →  stress = modulus × strain | Hooke's law per unit size | Dividing by area and length removes the size of the sample.
N O Ymod | Young's modulus | Y |
d Elastic modulus for tension or compression along one direction.
g Young’s modulus
R[def] Ymod <- elin : Y = (F⊥/A)/(ΔL/L₀) | Tensile modulus | Ratio of tensile stress to tensile strain.
N O Bmod | Bulk modulus | B = −Δp/(ΔV/V₀) |
d Elastic modulus for uniform compression; its inverse is the compressibility.
g bulk modulus; compressibility
R[def] Bmod <- elin, stress : B = −Δp V₀/ΔV | Volume modulus | Bulk stress is the change of pressure Δp.
N O Smod | Shear modulus | S |
d Elastic modulus for shear deformation.
g shear modulus
R[def] Smod <- elin : S = (F∥/A)/(Δx/L₀) | Shear modulus | Ratio of shear stress to shear strain.

N O pres | Pressure | p = F⊥/A |
d Perpendicular force per unit area; a scalar. SI unit the pascal, 1 Pa = 1 N/m².
g pressure; normal pressure; pascal (Pa)
R[def] pres <- F, L : p = F⊥/A | Normal force per area | Same in all directions at a point of a fluid at rest.
§ 1 12.4
N M ssdiag | Stress–strain diagram | σ(ε) |
d A material is linear up to the proportionality limit, elastic (returns to shape) up to the elastic limit, plastic beyond it, and breaks at the ultimate stress.
p linear region = Hooke's law; elastic deformation reversible; plastic deformation permanent; fracture at breaking stress
g stress-strain diagram; elastic; elastic limit; linearity limit (proportionality limit); plastic behavior; breaking stress (ultimate stress)
R[meas] ssdiag <- stress, strain, elin : measured stress versus strain | Material test | Linear elasticity is the initial straight part.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Chapter 13
§ 1 13.1
N O G | Gravitational constant | G | prim
d Strength of gravity between two masses, 6.674 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg², first measured with a torsion balance (Cavendish).
g universal gravitational constant
N M ngrav | Newton's law of gravitation | F = Gm₁m₂/r² | ax
d Every pair of point masses attracts along the line joining them with a force proportional to both masses and inversely to the square of the distance.
p inverse square; central and attractive; superposition holds; a spherical shell acts as a point mass at its centre
g Newton’s law of gravitation
R[law] F <- ngrav, G, m, r : F⃗₁₂ = −G m₁m₂/r² r̂₁₂ | Force between masses | A force law, used as the F⃗ in the second law.

§ 1 13.2
N O gfield | Gravitational field | g⃗ = F⃗/m |
d Gravitational force per unit mass at a point; it exists whether or not a test mass is there.
g gravitational field
R[def] gfield <- ngrav, G : g⃗(r⃗) = −GM/r² r̂ | Force per test mass | Removes the test mass from the force law.
R[thm] g <- gfield : g = G M_E/R_E² | Free-fall acceleration explained | The measured g of Chapter 3 follows from Earth's mass and radius.
N O wapp | Apparent weight | w_app |
d What a scale reads: the normal force, which differs from mg in an accelerating or rotating frame (reduced at the equator by Earth's rotation).
g apparent weight
R[thm] wapp <- n2, w, noninert : w_app = N = m(g − a) | Scale in an accelerating frame | The scale measures the support force, not gravity alone.

§ 1 13.3
N O Ugr | Gravitational potential energy | U = −GMm/r |
d Potential energy of two masses at any separation, with zero at infinite separation; near Earth's surface it reduces to mgy.
R[thm] Ugr <- ngrav, U, cons : U(r) = −∫_∞^r F_r dr′ = −GMm/r, with F_r = −GMm/r′² | Work from infinity | Gravity is conservative.
R[lim] Ugrav <- Ugr : −GMm/(R + y) ≈ −GMm/R + mgy for y ≪ R | Near the surface | Expanding to first order in y/R.
N O vesc | Escape velocity | v_esc = √(2GM/R) |
d Minimum launch speed for a body to move off to infinite distance.
g escape velocity
R[thm] vesc <- econs, Ugr, K : ½mv² − GMm/R = 0 | Energy just zero | Kinetic energy that exactly cancels the potential energy.
N S bound | Gravitationally bound orbit | E = K + U < 0 |
d A system whose total energy is negative cannot separate to infinity; the bodies stay within a maximum distance.
m trajectory confined to r ≤ r_max; total energy E < 0 (E = −GMm/2r on a circle)
g gravitationally bound
R[thm] bound <- Ugr, Emech : E = ½mv² − GMm/r < 0 ⇒ r ≤ GMm/|E| | Negative energy | K ≥ 0 forces U ≤ E.

§ 1 13.4
N O vorb | Orbital speed | v = √(GM/r) |
d Speed of a circular orbit at radius r; independent of the satellite's mass.
g orbital speed
R[thm] vorb <- ngrav, Fc : GMm/r² = mv²/r | Gravity supplies the centripetal force | Solved for v.
N O Torb | Orbital period | T = 2π√(r³/GM) |
d Time for one circular orbit.
g orbital period
R[thm] Torb <- vorb : T = 2πr/v | Circumference over speed | With v = √(GM/r).

§ 1 13.5
N M kep1 | Kepler's first law | orbit = ellipse, Sun at a focus |
d Every planet moves on an ellipse with the Sun at one focus; derived by Newton from the inverse-square law.
p conic sections α/r = 1 + e cos θ; e < 1 bound ellipse, e ≥ 1 unbound; empirical (Kepler) then derived (Newton)
g Kepler’s first law
R[thm] kep1 <- ngrav, n2, bound : inverse-square central force with E < 0 ⇒ ellipse | Solving the equation of motion | Bound inverse-square orbits are ellipses.
N S orbit | Elliptical orbit | r(θ) = α/(1 + e cos θ) |
d The orbital state: an ellipse traced periodically, closest to the Sun at perihelion and farthest at aphelion.
m conic section with eccentricity e < 1, semimajor axis a; closed periodic curve
g perihelion; aphelion
R[read] orbit <- kep1, rt : r(θ) = α/(1 + e cos θ) | Trajectory shape | The trajectory of a bound planet.
N M kep2 | Kepler's second law | dA/dt = const |
d The line from Sun to planet sweeps equal areas in equal times, so a planet moves fastest at perihelion.
p equivalent to conservation of angular momentum for a central force; holds for any central force
g Kepler’s second law
R[thm] kep2 <- Lcons, orbit : dA/dt = L/(2m) | Central force ⇒ constant L | Area swept per time is proportional to angular momentum.
N M kep3 | Kepler's third law | T² = 4π²a³/(GM) |
d The square of the orbital period is proportional to the cube of the semimajor axis.
p same constant for all planets of one star; independent of planet mass (when m ≪ M)
g Kepler’s third law
R[gen] kep3 <- Torb, orbit : circular radius r → semimajor axis a | From circles to ellipses | The circular-orbit period generalised.

§ 1 13.6
N O Ftide | Tidal force | ΔF ≈ 2GMmΔr/r³ |
d Difference in the gravitational pull across a body; it stretches the body along the line to the attracting mass. Sun and Moon aligned give spring tides, at right angles neap tides.
g tidal force; spring tide; neap tide
R[thm] Ftide <- ngrav : ΔF = |dF/dr| Δr = 2GMmΔr/r³ | Gradient of the inverse-square force | First-order difference across the body.

§ 1 13.7
N O c | Speed of light | c | prim
d Speed of light in vacuum, 2.998 × 10⁸ m/s (exact by the definition of the metre).
N M GR | General relativity | G_μν = 8πG T_μν / c⁴ | rel
d Gravity is the curvature of space-time produced by mass and energy; free bodies follow the straightest paths of the curved geometry.
p geometric (non-Euclidean) space-time; no action at a distance; reduces to Newton's law for weak fields and slow motion
g theory of general relativity; space-time; non-Euclidean geometry; action-at-a-distance force
N M equiv | Principle of equivalence | gravity ≡ acceleration (locally) | rel ax
d No local experiment can distinguish a uniform gravitational field from an accelerated frame; a freely falling frame is locally inertial.
p local statement; gravitational mass = inertial mass; free fall replaces the inertial frame
g principle of equivalence
R[gen] GR <- equiv, ngrav, c : curvature ↔ energy density;  weak field → ∇²Φ = 4πGρ | Gravity as geometry | Equivalence makes gravity a property of space-time; Newton's law fixes the weak-field limit.
R[lim] ngrav <- GR : weak field, v ≪ c ⇒ F = GMm/r² | Newtonian limit | Newton's law is the slow, weak-field limit of general relativity.
N S bh | Black hole | r < R_S | rel
d A mass compressed inside its Schwarzschild radius; nothing, not even light, escapes from inside the event horizon.
m space-time region bounded by the event horizon r = R_S
g black hole
N O RS | Schwarzschild radius | R_S = 2GM/c² | rel
d Radius at which the escape velocity from a mass M reaches the speed of light; it marks the event horizon.
g Schwarzschild radius; event horizon
R[thm] RS <- GR, G, c : R_S = 2GM/c² | Horizon of a point mass | From the Schwarzschild solution of general relativity.
R[thm] bh <- RS, GR : collapse to r < R_S | Collapse inside the horizon | Light cones inside point inward.
X neutron star :: an astrophysical example (compact star) without a separate model in the chapter
